{
  "id": "earth-003",
  "title": "How does the water cycle work?",
  "category": "earth-science",
  "subject_area": "water on Earth",
  "difficulty_level": "introductory",
  "target_audience": [
    "general public",
    "primary school",
    "secondary school"
  ],
  "central_question": "Where does rain come from, and where does the water go afterwards?",
  "template": "principle",
  "principle_statement": "Earth's water is always moving and changing form: the Sun's energy evaporates it, it condenses into clouds, falls as precipitation, and runs or soaks back toward the oceans.",
  "domain_of_validity": "The natural global water cycle, without direct human use of water.",
  "scientific_explanation": {
    "level_1": "The water on Earth keeps moving in a big circle. The Sun warms the oceans, and some water turns into an invisible gas called water vapor. High in the sky it is cold, so the vapor turns back into tiny drops that make clouds. When the drops grow heavy, they fall as rain or snow. The water then runs downhill or soaks into the ground, and much of it ends up back in the ocean.",
    "level_2": "Evaporation turns liquid and frozen water into water vapor, using the Sun's energy, and most of it comes from the oceans. Warm air carries the vapor upward, where the cold makes it condense into tiny droplets that form clouds. When droplets combine into heavier drops, precipitation falls as rain, snow or hail. Most of the rain runs off the land into streams and rivers, and some seeps into the ground. Plants also release water through their leaves.",
    "level_3": "The water cycle is a system of storage and flow. For any place, the change in the stored water S equals the water flowing in, I, minus the water flowing out, O, so S = I - O. Over the oceans evaporation is greater than precipitation, while over land precipitation usually exceeds evaporation, and the difference returns to the oceans through runoff and groundwater discharge. A water molecule that evaporates spends about 10 days in the air."
  },
  "supporting_evidence": [
    "A water molecule spends about 10 days in the air after it evaporates.",
    "Over 96 percent of Earth's water is in the oceans, and about 68 percent of Earth's fresh water is locked up in ice, snow and glaciers."
  ],
